      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "ἀνθρώπων; ἀρχαίων τάφους",
          "english_quote": "The adjective modifies “tombs” across the printed punctuation, while the following genitive identifies the ancient people whose tombs are meant.",
          "issue": "The grammatical explanation is incorrect. ἀρχαίων is genitive plural and agrees with ἀνθρώπων, not with accusative plural τάφους. The main rendering “the tombs of ancient people” correctly represents that relationship.",
          "suggested_revision": "Replace the rationale with: “ἀρχαίων agrees with ἀνθρώπων in the genitive plural; together they identify whose tombs are meant. The English reads the wording continuously across the selected edition’s question mark, as disclosed in the punctuation note.” Retain the supplied punctuation in the source transcription.",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "ἐπὶ τῇ ἀχρήστῳ θρησκείᾳ ἑαυτῶν κατεγνωκότες",
          "english_quote": "thereby passing judgment against themselves over their useless worship",
          "issue": "“Thereby” makes the theft the means of passing judgment, while the perfect participle κατεγνωκότες presents the priests as having already reached or standing in that adverse judgment. The rationale likewise substitutes an inference about what their theft exposes for the participle’s stated relationship to the action.",
          "suggested_revision": "Use “having passed judgment against themselves over their useless worship” and adjust the accompanying rationale to preserve the perfect participle rather than asserting that the judgment is produced by the theft. A source-ordered revision of the question could place this participial phrase before “secretly remove many of the votive offerings.”",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
